Integrating GraphQL with WebSockets allows for efficient, real-time data fetching and updates in your hybrid app. By utilizing GraphQL subscriptions, you can establish a two-way communication channel between the React Native front-end and the Flutter AI backend. This enables instant updates and event-driven interactions, enhancing the user experience and responsiveness of your application.

**GraphQL Subscriptions in React Native**:

1. **Define GraphQL Subscriptions**:
   - In your GraphQL schema, define subscription types for the data that will be pushed in real-time:
   ```graphql
   type Prediction {
     id: ID!
     text: String!
   }

   type Subscription {
     newPredictions(inputText: String!): [Prediction]
   }
   ```
2. **Create a GraphQL Client with Subscription Support**:
   - In your React Native app, use the `apollo-client` library and its `useSubscription` hook to create a subscription:
   ```jsx
   import { useSubscription, gql } from '@apollo/client';

   const NEW_PREDICTIONS_SUB = gql`
     subscription NewPredictions($inputText: String!) {
       newPredictions(inputText: $inputText) {
         id
         text
       }
     }
   `;

   function AiAdvisor() {
     const [inputText, setInputText] = useState('');
     const [predictions, setPredictions] = useState([]);

     const { data, error, loading } = useSubscription(
       NEW_PREDICTIONS_SUB,
       {
         variables: { inputText },
       }
     );

     if (loading) return <Text>Loading...</Text>;
     if (error) return <Text>Error: {error.message}</Text>;

     if (data) setPredictions(data.newPredictions);

     return (
       <View>
         <Text>AiAdvisor</Text>
         <TextInput
           value={inputText}
           onChangeText={(text) => setInputText(text)}
           placeholder="Type to get AI suggestions"
         />
         <Button title="Submit" onPress={(event) => {
           event.preventDefault();
           // Send input to the WebSocket server via this WebSocket client instance
           const wsClient = new ChatClient();
           wsClient.send(inputText);
         }} />
         <Text>Predictions: {predictions.map((prediction) => prediction.text).join(', ')}</Text>
       </View>
     );
   }
   ```

**Handling GraphQL Subscriptions in Flutter**:

1. **Expose GraphQL Subscriptions in the Backend**:
   - In your Flutter AI backend, expose the GraphQL subscription types using the `graphql` package:
   ```dart
   import 'package:graphql.dart' as gql;
   import 'package:office_flutter/office_flutter.dart';

   final schema = gql.GraphQLSchema(
     typeDefs: '''
       type Prediction {
         id: ID!
         text: String!
       }

       type Subscription {
         newPredictions(inputText: String!): [Prediction]
       }
     ''',
     resolvers: getResolvers(),
   );

   Future<GraphQLResponse> subscribe(String inputText) async {
     final query = '''
       subscription NewPredictions($inputText: String!) {
         newPredictions(inputText: $inputText) {
           id
           text
         }
       }
     ''';
     final variables = {'inputText': inputText};

     return await schema.execute(query, variables: variables);
   }
   ```

2. **Start GraphQL Subscriptions in the Backend**:
   - In your Flutter app, when a client submits an input, use the `subscribe` function to start the GraphQL subscription and receive real-time predictions:
   ```dart
